cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this registry, please cite it as below. Always verify any visit against the linked manuscript scan before citing the document itself; rows are status=draft until reviewed, and reviewed until leaf-verified."
title: "California Ship Registry, 1769–1846: A Documentary Register of Vessel Visits to the Californias"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Coyne
    given-names: Aodhan
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-8630-3768"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Aodhanm/california-ship-registry"
url: "https://ships.archivesofcalifornia.com/"
abstract: >-
  The first machine-readable registry of documented vessel visits to the
  Californias, from the founding of Alta California (1769) to the eve of the
  gold rush. The unit of record is the visit (one vessel, one anchorage,
  one time), each row carrying its evidence as manuscript-leaf citations with
  deep links to the corresponding page scans. Seeded from the Archives of
  California calendar of the Savage transcripts (BANC MSS C-A) and corroborated
  against H. H. Bancroft's History of California and Adele Ogden's trading-vessel
  itineraries. The dataset publishes its refusals: a taxonomy of seven false-ship
  classes (policy phantoms, era-conflations, person- and place-names, OCR
  garbles, mis-flaggings, retrospective mentions) documents how a non-ship gets
  minted from manuscript calendars and OCR'd print, with the guards that catch
  each. Every ship traces to a reliable source; entries are graded draft →
  reviewed → verified.
keywords:
  - California history
  - maritime history
  - Alta California
  - Spanish borderlands
  - Mexican California
  - maritime fur trade
  - hide and tallow trade
  - Pacific shipping
  - digital humanities
  - record linkage
